Z||000000||Philphine||11-11-2001||04:50 PM||||<BR>or it broke. the threaded part i think is still in the hole. guessing the exhaust is clogged, but it didn't seem like it by how it ran. i'm not even sure exactly when it happened.<P>for now my immediate problem is i can't find the lead. it's my gt and i'm still not very good at where things are and there's not another around to look at. could someone give me an idea where the lead comes from. for all i know it shot it out and ripped it away. i can't find it feeling in the area. maybe jack it up and look under it?<P>also if the lead is broken and i have to repair it, is there anything special about it? like some kind of resistance lead?<P>if i can't find it right off i still want to put the sensor back. i'm kind of worried about losing a new one too. dose the 4cyl one fit, just to plug the hole? i have a spare i think, that i can use to see if it will blow too and have there at least until i find the lead. thanks for any help.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bazooka||11-11-2001||06:50 PM||||Phil,    The O2 sensor is a single wire lead that goes back to the ECM inside by the rear console. Its routing would be along the main wiring harness thru the firewall. The O2 sensor has a permanently attached lead about 15 inches long and has a quick connector at the other end. The GM single wire sensors are interchangeable. Its Delco Part 25162693.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-11-2001||08:08 PM||||The car half of the lead is nothing special but you must solder any splices you make. The connector is a standard single pin Weather Pack piece and you can get them thru anyplace that caries MotorMite products.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||11-12-2001||01:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>dose the 4cyl one fit, just to plug the hole? i have a spare i think, that i can use to see if it will blow too and have there at least until i find the lead. thanks for any help.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, the O2 sensors for the V6 and both 4 cylinder engines are the same part, same connector.  The bosch ones run about $20 at autozones.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||11-12-2001||04:13 PM||||it just got ugly.<P>i found the lead. it comes off the wire bundle really low and disconnected almost hangs to the ground. not broken though. the other end of the o2 was still hanging off it.<P>but when i tried to put in the new one i either striped the threads putting it in crooked or the old was was in crooked. it didn't come out too easy.<P>short of trying to pull the pipe out to get to it better or weld a new bung on, is there anything i can do? i stopped by napa to see if they had anything but the closest was a sparkplug hole chaser, but it's the wrong thread. if i can find out what the thread is maybe i can run a tap through it and get lucky. at work now so i'll have to wait till tomorrow to try more.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||11-12-2001||08:02 PM||||Hey Phil!<BR>It should be the same as a spark plug (metric), so a chaser (beer?) might help out...<BR>Get a GOOD replacement O2 sensor, as the Bosch are not reliable, I have had 2 failures with the Bosch type...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||11-12-2001||09:05 PM||||nope wrecker, it's bigger. unless there's a bigger sparkplug size i don't know about. already got a replacement. bosch. eh...it's what they had.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||11-13-2001||04:41 PM||||my apologies fierowrecker. and happy ones at that. went to another shop and they had one with what looks like the right size. at work again so i'll try tomorrow morning. got my fingers crossed.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||11-13-2001||08:35 PM||||Getting a good brand is important. I was stuck when my O2 went bad and I was going in for smog. I went to Pep Boys, grabbed a Borg/Warner, and passed smog. When I got home I got a code again and this time it was because the sensor melted! No plugged cat, (because there was no cat) just a little piece of melted plastic at the end! <P>Went to the dealer and picked up my AC/Delco O2 sensor and went on my merry way. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||11-14-2001||06:24 PM||||calling it good ...for now.<P>used the chaser, got the o2 in, found the lead and connected it. put about 20mls on it (quick roundabout and drive to work), no codes and running pretty good. did a couple of miles on the highway and still no codes, i had been getting one one i got up to highway speed and thinking egr. i'll go nome on the highway (about 15mls) and see what happens. thanks for the help y'all.||||reg||1
